> ## 6 October 2026 : HOW to get a good looking Cycles viewport in Solaris (recipe, step by step) (12 October 2026, 03:02:52 +0200)
>
> - 6 October 2026 : HOW THE WHITE SCREEN WAS SOLVED (repeat of the fix, for a quick find): (1) switch the viewport to Cycles (the label at the top of the viewport, "Cycles Persp"); (2) capture the Houdini window with `screencapture -l <windowid>` (`Scripts/CaptureHoudiniViewport.sh`) to SEE the viewport, a flipbook cannot; (3) test a bare sphere in `/stage`: it rendered, so the engine was fine; (4) put the display flag on the nodes of the chain ONE AT A TIME (`lt_assign`, `lt_dome`, `lt_key`, `lt_karma`): the viewport went white only with the display flag on `lt_karma` (the Karma Render Settings LOP); (5) leave the display flag on `lt_key` (or any node BEFORE `lt_karma`); `lt_render` still renders through its LOP Path `/stage/lt_karma`. (12 October 2026, 03:02:52 +0200)
> - 6 October 2026 : WHY THE CYCLES VIEW LOOKED UGLY AND HOW IT WAS FIXED. (a) Cycles reads the material PREVIEW network (`UsdPreviewSurface`), not the Karma Principled shader. The preview normal map, read the wrong way, drew black fibres on the petals and black patches on the leaves: a Python LOP `lt_cycles_preview_fix` (placed after `lt_assign`, before `lt_camera`) clears the `inputs:normal` connection of every UsdPreviewSurface; Karma keeps its own normal map. (b) Lights: Cycles is much more sensitive than Karma and the dome is the VISIBLE background, so the dome sets the background grey: dome intensity 0.25 and exposure -1.3 gives a mid grey; key light exposure -0.8 with angle 6 (soft shadows); a FILL light `lt_fill` (copy of the key, rotation y + 160, x -15, exposure -1.9) lights the sides in shadow. Karma values to restore for Karma: dome 1.0 / 0, key 1.5. (12 October 2026, 03:02:52 +0200)
> - 6 October 2026 : WHERE the Cycles settings are. There is NO Cycles node in Solaris (searched all node types for "cycles"): the delegate is configured by attributes on the render settings prim, `cycles:samples`, `cycles:device`, `cycles:threads`, `cycles:time_limit`, `cycles:integrator:*` (max_bounce and others, `use_denoise`, `use_adaptive_sampling`, `use_light_tree`); the viewport quality is also set from the viewport's own render options (the small pencil icon next to the "Cycles Persp" label). To author them in the graph: an Edit Properties or Python LOP that sets the attribute on `/Render/rendersettings`. Not done yet: the display flag must stay BEFORE `lt_karma`, so such a node would have to sit before it. (12 October 2026, 03:02:52 +0200)
